# --- grammar / sentence correction
E("Sentence Correction","Moderate",VP,2025,[2026],"Choose the sentence that is grammatically correct.",["Walking down the street, the trees seemed to sway in the wind.","Walking down the street, I noticed the trees swaying in the wind.","Walking down the street, the wind caused the trees to sway.","Walking down the street, the swaying trees were noticed."],"B","The opening participle phrase must modify the person walking. Only (B) has 'I' as the subject; the others leave the modifier dangling.",N),
E("Subject-Verb Agreement","Moderate",VP,2026,[],"Choose the sentence that is grammatically correct.",["Neither of the boys have done his homework.","Neither of the boys has done his homework.","Neither of the boys have done their homeworks.","Neither of the boy has done his homework."],"B","'Neither' is singular, so it takes 'has'; 'homework' is uncountable, and 'of the boys' needs the plural 'boys'.",N),
E("Tenses","Easy",VP,2026,[],"Choose the sentence that is grammatically correct.",["She has been living here since five years.","She is living here for five years.","She has been living here for five years.","She lives here since five years."],"C","'For' is used with a period of time; 'since' needs a starting point; the present perfect continuous fits an action continuing up to now.",N),
E("Sentence Correction","Moderate",GP,2025,[2026],"Identify the sentence with a grammatical error.",["The new employee, along with the rest of the team, were responsible for the project's failure.","Having studied English for five years, I find the grammar rules easy.","The report, whose findings were groundbreaking, was published in a prestigious journal.","By the time the manager arrived, the employees had been working on the project for three hours."],"A","The subject is the singular 'employee' ('along with...' does not make it plural), so the verb should be 'was'.","Adapted from the 2025/2026 pattern; option (B) was rewritten because the original version contained a second error, so this is not counted as a past-paper item."),
E("Sentence Correction","Easy",VP,2026,[2025],"Identify the sentence with a semantic error.",["The dog chased its tail with a spoon.","The sun is shining in the sky.","The author is writing the book.","The rain is falling downwards."],"A","A dog chasing its tail 'with a spoon' is illogical; the other sentences are meaningful.",N),
E("Grammar","Easy",VP,2023,[],"Which of the following sentences is grammatically correct?",["I and my friends went to the movie.","My friends and I went to the movie.","Me and my friends went to the movie.","My friends and me went to the movie."],"B","'My friends and I' is the correct subject form, with the speaker named last.",N),
E("Grammar","Easy",VP,2023,[],"Which of the following is an example of a subordinate clause?",["I like pizza","Because I like pizza","Pizza is my favorite food","I ate pizza for dinner"],"B","A subordinate clause cannot stand alone; 'Because I like pizza' needs a main clause.",N),
E("Grammar","Easy",VP,2023,[],"Which sentence uses the correct adjective form?",["The movie was more scarier than I expected","The movie was scarier than I expected","The movie was scare than I expected","The movie was scary than I expected"],"B","The comparative of 'scary' is 'scarier'; 'more scarier' is a double comparative.",N),
E("Grammar","Easy",VP,2023,[],"Choose the correct sentence:",["All that is glitters is not gold","All that is glittering is not gold","All that glitters is not gold","All which glitters is not gold"],"C","The proverb is 'All that glitters is not gold'.",N),
E("Sentence Structure","Moderate",VP,2023,[],"\"Although they are 250 miles apart, they keep in constant contact on the internet.\" This is a:",["Simple sentence","Compound sentence","Complex sentence","Compound-complex sentence"],"C","One subordinate clause ('Although...') plus one main clause makes a complex sentence.",N),
